{%- comment -%}
  GOD SQUAD — Facets
  Phase 13. Shopify's native storefront filtering, rendered once.

  Accepts:
  - results   {Object} required. A collection (or, later, a search) — anything
                       carrying .filters. Written against `results` rather than
                       `collection` so the search page can render it unchanged.
  - form_id   {String} required. The id of the ONE form on this surface. Every
                       control here is bound to it by the HTML form attribute,
                       including the sort select, which lives outside this
                       snippet in the toolbar.
  - id_prefix {String} optional, default 'Facet'. Scopes generated element ids.

  Usage:
  {% render 'facets', results: collection, form_id: form_id %}

  ONE FORM, ONE SET OF CONTROLS.

  The filter controls are rendered EXACTLY ONCE in the document. At --bp-md and
  above they are a horizontal row of dropdowns above the toolbar; below it the
  same element is a fixed drawer. That is a CSS difference, not a second copy.

  Themes that render a desktop sidebar and a mobile drawer separately have to
  keep two sets of checkboxes in step, and submit both. Here there is one
  checked state, one submitted value per choice, and nothing to synchronise.

  WHY A SIDEBAR WAS REJECTED, IN NUMBERS.

  snippets/grid-sizes.liquid derives the card slot from the row left after
  chrome. Today chrome_d is 96, so at the 1440 container the capped row is 1344
  and (1344 + 32) / 304 = 4 columns fit. A 240px sidebar plus a 48px gap makes
  chrome_d 384: the capped row is 1056 and only 3 columns fit — and at the
  1200px container a merchant may set, only 2. A sidebar narrow enough to keep
  four columns is about 112px, which is narrower than the word "Availability".
  The horizontal bar costs one control height plus one gap, once, and leaves
  every number in grid-sizes untouched.

  APPLY ON CONFIRM, EXCEPT FOR LINKS.

  Checkboxes and the price fields apply on a visible submit. sections/
  main-collection.liquid already settled this for the sort control and stated
  the reason: submit-on-change is a WCAG 2.2 SC 3.2.2 On Input failure at Level
  A, and it is worst for a keyboard user arrowing through options, who triggers
  a navigation on every option they pass. A checkbox that auto-submits is the
  same defect under a different control.

  The active-filter chips and Clear all are LINKS, so they act immediately.
  That is not an inconsistency: SC 3.2.2 governs changing the setting of a
  control, and activating a link is not that. Do not "harmonise" them into the
  form.

  NO JAVASCRIPT IS REQUIRED.

  Shopify's filter parameters are ordinary query-string values, and a checkbox
  named filter.v.option.size with value L submits exactly what Shopify's own
  url_to_add would have produced. With scripting off this renders in flow above
  the grid as a stack of collapsed groups, and every control still works. The
  only thing script adds is the drawer.

  Also: form controls inside a CLOSED <details> are still submitted, so
  collapsing a group never silently drops what is checked inside it.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign id_prefix = id_prefix | default: 'Facet'

  comment
    A group with one value is not a choice. Shopify returns one whenever a
    collection happens to hold a single vendor or a single product type, and
    rendering it gives the customer a control whose only effect is to filter
    nothing out. Dropped here rather than in the section, so every caller gets
    the same rule.

    A price_range group is kept regardless: its "values" array is empty by
    design, because its controls come from min_value, max_value and range_max.
  endcomment
  assign usable = 0
  for f in results.filters
    if f.type == 'price_range'
      assign usable = usable | plus: 1
    elsif f.values.size > 1
      assign usable = usable | plus: 1
    endif
  endfor

  assign active_count = 0
  for f in results.filters
    assign active_count = active_count | plus: f.active_values.size
    if f.type == 'price_range'
      if f.min_value.value != blank or f.max_value.value != blank
        assign active_count = active_count | plus: 1
      endif
    endif
  endfor
-%}

{%- if usable > 0 -%}
  <div class="facets" data-facets>
    {%- comment -%}
      The drawer's own header. Present at every width but only visible below
      --bp-md, because above it the panel is a row in the page rather than a
      layer over it.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="facets__bar">
      <p class="facets__bar-title">{{ 'collection.filters.title' | t }}</p>
      <button
        type="button"
        class="facets__close"
        data-facets-close
      >
        {% render 'icon-close' %}
        <span class="visually-hidden">{{ 'collection.filters.close' | t }}</span>
      </button>
    </div>

    <div class="facets__groups">
      {%- for f in results.filters -%}
        {%- liquid
          assign renderable = false
          if f.type == 'price_range'
            assign renderable = true
          elsif f.values.size > 1
            assign renderable = true
          endif
        -%}
        {%- if renderable -%}
          {%- comment -%}
            Open when something inside it is applied, closed otherwise. One
            rule, correct at both widths: a row of closed dropdowns on desktop,
            a compact accordion on a phone, and whatever is active is visible.

            <details> and <summary> are the whole disclosure. No aria-expanded,
            no aria-controls, no role — the same reasoning sections/
            main-product.liquid records for its description accordion.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <details
            class="facets__group"
            {% if f.active_values.size > 0 %}open{% endif %}
          >
            <summary class="facets__summary">
              <span class="facets__summary-label">{{ f.label }}</span>
              {%- if f.active_values.size > 0 -%}
                <span class="facets__summary-count">{{ f.active_values.size }}</span>
              {%- endif -%}
              {% render 'icon-chevron' %}
            </summary>

            <div class="facets__values">
              {%- case f.type -%}
                {%- when 'price_range' -%}
                  {%- comment -%}
                    Shopify's own price filter: two parameters, gte and lte, in
                    the store's currency. The bounds are whole currency units,
                    which is what Shopify expects here — no custom arithmetic,
                    and nothing client-side deciding what is in range.

                    range_max arrives in cents, so it is divided for the
                    placeholder and the max attribute.
                  {%- endcomment -%}
                  {%- assign price_ceiling = f.range_max | divided_by: 100 -%}
                  <div class="facets__price">
                    <div class="facets__price-field">
                      <label for="{{ id_prefix }}-price-min">
                        {{ 'collection.filters.price_from' | t }}
                      </label>
                      <input
                        id="{{ id_prefix }}-price-min"
                        form="{{ form_id }}"
                        class="facets__price-input"
                        type="number"
                        inputmode="numeric"
                        name="{{ f.min_value.param_name | default: 'filter.v.price.gte' }}"
                        value="{{ f.min_value.value }}"
                        min="0"
                        max="{{ price_ceiling }}"
                        placeholder="0"
                      >
                    </div>
                    <div class="facets__price-field">
                      <label for="{{ id_prefix }}-price-max">
                        {{ 'collection.filters.price_to' | t }}
                      </label>
                      <input
                        id="{{ id_prefix }}-price-max"
                        form="{{ form_id }}"
                        class="facets__price-input"
                        type="number"
                        inputmode="numeric"
                        name="{{ f.max_value.param_name | default: 'filter.v.price.lte' }}"
                        value="{{ f.max_value.value }}"
                        min="0"
                        max="{{ price_ceiling }}"
                        placeholder="{{ price_ceiling }}"
                      >
                    </div>
                  </div>

                {%- else -%}
                  {%- comment -%}
                    list and boolean render identically: a checkbox per value,
                    named for the filter's own parameter and carrying Shopify's
                    own value. Checking two boxes in one group submits the
                    parameter twice, which is exactly the OR encoding Shopify
                    documents — so the no-script path produces the same URL the
                    scripted one would.

                    The count is the number of products the value would leave,
                    and it comes from Shopify. It is not rendered when Shopify
                    does not supply one.
                  {%- endcomment -%}
                  <ul class="facets__list" role="list">
                    {%- for v in f.values -%}
                      {%- assign box_id = id_prefix | append: '-' | append: forloop.parentloop.index | append: '-' | append: forloop.index -%}
                      <li class="facets__value">
                        <input
                          type="checkbox"
                          class="facets__checkbox visually-hidden"
                          id="{{ box_id }}"
                          form="{{ form_id }}"
                          name="{{ v.param_name }}"
                          value="{{ v.value }}"
                          {% if v.active %}checked{% endif %}
                        >
                        <label class="facets__label" for="{{ box_id }}">
                          {%- if f.presentation == 'swatch' and v.swatch.color != blank -%}
                            <span
                              class="facets__swatch"
                              style="--facet-swatch: {{ v.swatch.color }}"
                              aria-hidden="true"
                            ></span>
                          {%- endif -%}
                          <span class="facets__label-text">{{ v.label }}</span>
                          {%- if v.count != blank -%}
                            <span class="facets__count">{{ v.count }}</span>
                          {%- endif -%}
                        </label>
                      </li>
                    {%- endfor -%}
                  </ul>
              {%- endcase -%}
            </div>
          </details>
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      The drawer's footer. Apply submits the one form; Clear all is a link, so
      it needs no form. Hidden above --bp-md, where the toolbar's own submit
      does the same job and the chips carry the clearing.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="facets__footer">
      <button type="submit" form="{{ form_id }}" class="button button--primary button--full">
        {{ 'collection.filters.apply' | t }}
      </button>
      {%- if active_count > 0 -%}
        <a class="facets__clear" href="{{ results.url }}">
          {{ 'collection.filters.clear_all' | t }}
        </a>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
  </div>

  {%- comment -%}
    The applied filters, as removable chips. Links, not form controls — each one
    is Shopify's own url_to_remove, so removing a filter is a plain navigation
    that works with scripting off and leaves the browser's history honest.

    Rendered outside .facets so they stay on the page when the drawer is closed:
    a customer on a phone has to be able to see what is applied without opening
    anything.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if active_count > 0 -%}
    <div class="facets-active" data-facets-active>
      <h2 class="visually-hidden">{{ 'collection.filters.active_heading' | t }}</h2>
      <ul class="facets-active__list" role="list">
        {%- for f in results.filters -%}
          {%- for v in f.active_values -%}
            <li>
              <a class="facets-active__chip" href="{{ v.url_to_remove }}">
                <span class="facets-active__group">{{ f.label }}:</span>
                {{ v.label }}
                {% render 'icon-close' %}
                <span class="visually-hidden">
                  {{ 'collection.filters.remove' | t: value: v.label }}
                </span>
              </a>
            </li>
          {%- endfor -%}

          {%- if f.type == 'price_range' -%}
            {%- if f.min_value.value != blank or f.max_value.value != blank -%}
              <li>
                <a class="facets-active__chip" href="{{ f.url_to_remove }}">
                  <span class="facets-active__group">{{ f.label }}:</span>
                  {%- if f.min_value.value != blank -%}
                    {{ f.min_value.value | times: 100 | money }}
                  {%- else -%}
                    {{ 0 | money }}
                  {%- endif -%}
                  &ndash;
                  {%- if f.max_value.value != blank -%}
                    {{ f.max_value.value | times: 100 | money }}
                  {%- else -%}
                    {{ f.range_max | money }}
                  {%- endif -%}
                  {% render 'icon-close' %}
                  <span class="visually-hidden">
                    {{ 'collection.filters.remove' | t: value: f.label }}
                  </span>
                </a>
              </li>
            {%- endif -%}
          {%- endif -%}
        {%- endfor -%}

        <li>
          <a class="facets-active__clear" href="{{ results.url }}">
            {{ 'collection.filters.clear_all' | t }}
          </a>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}
